# Oil, water and colour: which liquid gets the dye?
#
# A safe density-and-mixing activity with water, vegetable oil and food
# colouring. Do not taste lab materials; protect clothes and surfaces because
# food colouring stains, and work with an adult.
register lv1

# Colour the water first, then add oil. Predict whether the yellow oil will
# change colour or form its own layer.
add v1 water 100mL
add v1 food_colour_blue 1mL
add v1 vegetable_oil 50mL
inspect v1

# Now reverse the order in a narrow cylinder. Food colouring is an aqueous
# dropper solution: it does not dye the oil, and its tiny coloured water phase
# settles below the oil instead.
new cylinder
add v2 vegetable_oil 50mL
add v2 food_colour_red 1mL
inspect v2

# The engine conserves generic vegetable oil as an unresolved mixture and
# draws the computed persistent layers. It does not yet model falling drops,
# temporary emulsions, detergent emulsification or a lava-lamp tablet.
